# Task 2 – Choose the Data Representation

## Objective
To represent parking floors and slots using Java data types and arrays.

## Data Types
- int: Floor number, slot number and duration.
- String: Vehicle registration number.
- LocalDateTime: Vehicle entry time.
- double: Parking fee.

## Array Representation
A jagged two-dimensional array (String[][]) is used to represent parking slots. Each floor can have a different number of slots.

## Type Conversion
The expression (duration + 59) / 60 calculates the number of started hours using integer arithmetic.

Example:
90 minutes = 2 billable hours.

## Input Method
The Scanner class is used to read user input.

## Alternative Representation
A one-dimensional array can represent all parking slots, but index calculations are required when floors have different numbers of slots. An ArrayList<String[]> can store floors dynamically.

## Complexity Analysis
- Accessing a slot using its indexes: O(1)
- Searching for a free slot: O(N)
- Space complexity: O(N)

Here, N represents the total number of parking slots.

## Conclusion
A jagged two-dimensional array is suitable because different floors can have different numbers of parking slots.import java.util.Scanner;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

public class Task02 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        String[][] slots = new String[3][];
        slots[0] = new String[20];
        slots[1] = new String[50];
        slots[2] = new String[100];

        System.out.print("Enter vehicle number: ");
        String vehicleNo = sc.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter floor number: ");
        int floor = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter slot number: ");
        int slot = sc.nextInt();

        LocalDateTime entryTime = LocalDateTime.now();

        int duration = 90;
        int hours = (duration + 59) / 60;
        double ratePerHour = 30.0;
        double fee = hours * ratePerHour;

        if (floor >= 0 && floor < slots.length &&
            slot >= 0 && slot < slots[floor].length &&
            slots[floor][slot] == null) {

            slots[floor][slot] = vehicleNo;

            System.out.println("Vehicle Number: " + vehicleNo);
            System.out.println("Entry Time: " + entryTime);
            System.out.println("Parking Fee: Rs " + fee);
            System.out.println("Parking slot allocated successfully.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid or occupied parking slot.");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}